from common import lesson, lab, cite, term, out

LESSONS = [
lesson(13,4,'naming-maps','Naming maps: the same activation in Hugging Face, TransformerLens 4, and nnsight',
 'Three libraries, three vocabularies, one set of tensors. The table on this page was produced by running all three on the same ids and printing the differences.',
 '''<h2 id="loading">Loading the model three ways</h2>
<p>Hugging Face is the ground truth: <code>GPT2LMHeadModel.from_pretrained("gpt2")</code>, with <code>output_hidden_states=True</code> and <code>output_attentions=True</code> on the call. With transformers 5, attentions are returned only when the model is loaded with <code>attn_implementation="eager"</code>; the default fused kernel returns an empty tuple.</p>
<p>TransformerLens 4 removed the <code>HookedTransformer</code> class that older tutorials use. The replacement is a bridge over the Hugging Face model '''+cite('tl-migration')+''':</p>
<pre><code>from transformer_lens import TransformerBridge
bridge = TransformerBridge.boot_transformers("gpt2")
bridge.enable_compatibility_mode(no_processing=True)   # raw weights; omit no_processing for HookedTransformer numerics
logits, cache = bridge.run_with_cache(ids)
cache["blocks.5.hook_resid_post"]</code></pre>
<p>Compatibility mode's defaults fold layer norms, centre the writing weights, and centre the unembedding, reproducing what <code>HookedTransformer.from_pretrained</code> used to do; <code>no_processing=True</code> keeps the raw weights so that activations match Hugging Face '''+cite('tl-compat')+'''. The call rewrites weights in place and must not be repeated on the same bridge.</p>
<p>nnsight wraps the Hugging Face model and lets you name activations by the module path '''+cite('nnsight-quickstart')+''':</p>
<pre><code>from nnsight import TransformersModel
model = TransformersModel("gpt2", task="text-generation", device_map="cpu", dispatch=True)
with model.trace(ids):
    mlp5 = model.transformer.h[5].mlp.output.save()    # request modules in the order they run
    h5 = model.transformer.h[5].output.save()
    logits = model.lm_head.output.save()</code></pre>
<p>Three nnsight rules, all verified while writing this page: pass <code>task</code> explicitly when offline; call <code>.save()</code> on anything you want after the block; and request modules in execution order, since asking for <code>h[5].output</code> and then <code>h[5].mlp.output</code> raises <code>OutOfOrderError</code> '''+cite('nnsight-getting')+'''. A trace block must also run from a file or notebook cell; the course's tool is a file for that reason.</p>
<h2 id="the-table">The verified table</h2>
<p>The names command runs all three on the running prompt's ids, looks up each activation under each library's name, and prints the shape and the largest absolute difference against Hugging Face:</p>'''+term(['python labs/anatomy.py names'])+out('python labs/anatomy.py names', keep=slice(0, 13))+'''
<p>Every row agrees to 1e-5 or better. Reading the table:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Residual stream.</strong> Hugging Face's <code>hidden_states[i]</code> is the stream entering block <em>i</em>, so TransformerLens's <code>blocks.i.hook_resid_pre</code> is <code>hidden_states[i]</code> and <code>hook_resid_post</code> is <code>hidden_states[i+1]</code>. In nnsight, <code>h[i].input</code> and <code>h[i].output</code>.</li>
<li><strong>The last hidden state is not resid_post.11.</strong> <code>hidden_states[12]</code> is after <code>ln_f</code>; it matches <code>ln_final.hook_out</code> and <code>ln_f.output</code>, and differs from <code>blocks.11.hook_resid_post</code> by hundreds. The stream after block 11 is available from TransformerLens and from nnsight's <code>h[11].output</code>, but not from the Hugging Face tuple.</li>
<li><strong>Sub-block writes.</strong> Hugging Face exposes attention and MLP outputs only through hooks on the sub-modules; TransformerLens has <code>hook_attn_out</code> and <code>hook_mlp_out</code>; nnsight has <code>h[i].attn.output[0]</code> and <code>h[i].mlp.output</code>. The attention module returns a tuple (output, attention weights or None), hence the <code>[0]</code>; the MLP and the block return a plain tensor in transformers 5.20.2, so <code>h[i].output[0]</code> would index the batch axis and silently drop a dimension.</li>
<li><strong>Patterns and neurons.</strong> <code>attentions[i]</code>, <code>blocks.i.attn.hook_pattern</code>, and a hook on the attention module all give (batch, head, query, key). MLP activations after GELU are <code>blocks.i.mlp.hook_post</code> and <code>h[i].mlp.act.output</code>.</li>
<li><strong>Layer-norm hooks are pre-affine.</strong> The note line in the output shows that <code>ln_final.hook_normalized</code> times <code>ln_f.weight</code> plus <code>ln_f.bias</code> reproduces <code>hidden_states[12]</code>; <code>hook_normalized</code> itself does not. The same holds for every <code>ln1</code> and <code>ln2</code> hook.</li>
<li><strong>BOS.</strong> <code>bridge.to_tokens(text)</code> prepends <code>&lt;|endoftext|&gt;</code> (50256) by default, so a TransformerLens run on text has one more position than a Hugging Face run on the same text and every position index shifts by one. This page passed ids directly to all three libraries.</li></ul>
<h2 id="processing">What default processing changes</h2>
<p>Run the same command with TransformerLens's default compatibility mode and the residual and logit rows stop matching:</p>'''+term(['python labs/anatomy.py names --processing default'])+out('python labs/anatomy.py names --processing default', keep=slice(2, 16))+'''
<p>The last two lines explain the discrepancies. Logits differ by a per-position constant (108.9 at most) because of <code>center_unembed</code>, and the difference vanishes after removing each row's mean; log-softmax agrees to 3.8e-05. Residual vectors differ by their mean over d_model because of <code>center_writing_weights</code>, and agree to 4.9e-04 once both are centred; the next layer norm removes that mean anyway, so the model's function is unchanged. The attention pattern and the MLP activations match in both modes because folding and centering are exact rewrites. The full cache has 493 entries; the block-5 list at the bottom of the output is the catalogue of hook names for one block, produced by the installed package rather than copied from documentation '''+cite('tl-hooks')+'''.</p>
<div class="evals"><span class="label">In interpretability work</span><p>Record which library, which processing mode, and which tokenization convention produced every number in a write-up. "resid_post at layer 5, position 3" means different vectors under default and no-processing modes (differing by a mean component), and different positions under BOS and no-BOS tokenization. A result that reproduces only under one library's defaults is a result about the defaults until you have shown otherwise.</p></div>
<div class="pitfall"><span class="label">Pitfall</span><p>Reading an older tutorial's <code>HookedTransformer.from_pretrained("gpt2")</code> and trying to install an older TransformerLens into a shared environment to make it work. The bridge API gives the same cache keys; the migration guide maps the old names to the new ones '''+cite('tl-migration')+'''. If an older tutorial's numbers are from processed weights, use the default compatibility mode and expect raw-weight comparisons to differ as above.</p></div>''',
 'A colleague reports that "the layer-5 residual stream at the cat token has norm 85" using Hugging Face, and another gets 86 with TransformerLens default mode and "the same prompt." List the questions that must be answered before deciding whether these agree.',
 'Which stream: hidden_states[5] (entering block 5) or hidden_states[6] (after it)? Did TransformerLens prepend BOS, shifting "the cat token" by one position? Did default processing centre the writing weights, which changes the norm by removing the mean component? Were the same ids used (lesson 01)? Any of these can account for a 1-unit difference; until they are fixed, the two numbers are not measurements of the same quantity.',
 lab(['python labs/anatomy.py names --layer 0','python labs/anatomy.py names --layer 11'],
 'Run the table for block 0 and for block 11. At block 11 the Hugging Face column for the residual after the block says it is not exposed; confirm that TransformerLens and nnsight still agree with each other there.',
 'At layer 0 every row matches as at layer 5. At layer 11 the "residual after block" row compares nnsight\'s h[11].output with TransformerLens\'s blocks.11.hook_resid_post (the Hugging Face column cannot supply it), and they agree to about 1e-4; hidden_states[12] still matches ln_final.hook_out.'),
 ['tl-migration','tl-compat','tl-hooks','nnsight-quickstart','nnsight-getting'], kw='TransformerLens TransformerBridge hook names run_with_cache nnsight trace hidden_states naming map'),

lesson(14,4,'architectural-variants','Architectural variants: GPT-2 versus Llama-style models',
 'RMSNorm, rotary positions, SwiGLU, grouped-query attention, and no biases. Each is a small change to one lesson of this course, verified against the papers and the installed model code.',
 '''<h2 id="the-family">The family</h2>
<p>Most open models you will inspect after GPT-2 share a recipe that the LLaMA paper lays out in three sentences: pre-normalisation with RMSNorm, SwiGLU in place of ReLU, and rotary positional embeddings in place of absolute ones '''+cite('llama')+'''. The Qwen3 technical report describes its dense models with the same components plus grouped-query attention and QK-Norm, and with the QKV bias of Qwen2 removed '''+cite('qwen3')+'''. The installed Hugging Face code for both model families was read to confirm the exact operations '''+cite('hf-llama')+'''. Here is the map from this course's lessons to what changes:</p>
<table><tr><th>Lesson</th><th>GPT-2 small</th><th>Llama / Qwen3 dense</th></tr>
<tr><td>02 positions</td><td>learned wpe (1024, 768), added once at the input</td><td>no position vector in the stream; rotary rotation of q and k in every layer</td></tr>
<tr><td>04 normalisation</td><td>LayerNorm with weight and bias, pre-LN, ε = 1e-5</td><td>RMSNorm with weight only, pre-norm (input_layernorm, post_attention_layernorm)</td></tr>
<tr><td>05–06 attention</td><td>12 query heads, 12 key/value heads, biases on c_attn and c_proj</td><td>more query heads than key/value heads (Qwen3-0.6B: 16 and 8); no biases; Qwen3 normalises q and k per head</td></tr>
<tr><td>07–08 MLP</td><td>c_fc, GELU (tanh), c_proj; width 4d</td><td>gate_proj, up_proj, SiLU, down_proj; width from intermediate_size</td></tr>
<tr><td>09 unembedding</td><td>tied to wte</td><td>tied in Qwen3-0.6B to 4B, untied from 8B up (Table 1 of the report)</td></tr>
<tr><td>11 weight storage</td><td>Conv1D, (in, out)</td><td>nn.Linear, (out, in)</td></tr></table>
<h2 id="rmsnorm">RMSNorm</h2>
<p>Zhang and Sennrich proposed dropping LayerNorm's re-centering and normalising by the root mean square alone '''+cite('rmsnorm')+'''. The installed Llama code computes, in float32, <em>x</em> · rsqrt(mean(<em>x</em>²) + ε), then multiplies by a learned weight; there is no bias and no mean subtraction '''+cite('hf-llama')+'''. Lesson 04's demo computed it on [1, 3, 5, 7]: rms = √21 = 4.5826 and the output is [0.2182, 0.6547, 1.0911, 1.5275]. Two consequences for analysis: the all-ones direction is no longer deleted by normalisation, so a write along it does reach the next sub-block; and folding works the same way (scale the next matrix's rows by the weight), with no bias to absorb.</p>
<h2 id="rope">Rotary positions</h2>
<p>Su et al. encode position by rotating the query and key vectors so that their dot product depends on the relative distance between positions '''+cite('rope')+'''. In the installed code, <code>rotate_half</code> splits each head's 64 (or head_dim) coordinates into two halves and forms [−x₂, x₁]; then <code>q_embed = q·cos + rotate_half(q)·sin</code> with cos and sin depending on position and on a per-pair frequency; keys get the same treatment, values do not '''+cite('hf-llama')+'''. The dot product of a rotated query at position <em>i</em> with a rotated key at position <em>j</em> depends on <em>i</em> − <em>j</em>, not on either alone.</p>
<p>For interpretability this changes what the QK circuit is. In GPT-2 the score is <em>x<sub>i</sub> W_Q W_K</em>ᵀ <em>x<sub>j</sub></em>ᵀ, one fixed bilinear form. With RoPE a position-dependent rotation sits between <em>W_Q</em> and <em>W_K</em>ᵀ, so the effective QK matrix is different for each relative offset, and a "which directions does this head match?" analysis has to be done per offset or on the unrotated weights with the rotation treated separately. The OV circuit is untouched, and there is no <code>hook_pos_embed</code>; the position information enters inside attention.</p>
<h2 id="gqa">Grouped-query attention</h2>
<p>Ainslie et al. introduced grouped-query attention: an intermediate number of key/value heads, more than one but fewer than the number of query heads '''+cite('gqa')+'''. Qwen3-0.6B has 16 query heads and 8 key/value heads '''+cite('qwen3')+'''. In code, <code>repeat_kv</code> expands the key and value tensors from (b, n_kv, s, d_head) to (b, n_q, s, d_head) by repeating each key/value head for the query heads in its group '''+cite('hf-llama')+'''. The attention pattern tensor still has one row-matrix per query head, shape (b, n_q, s, s); two query heads in the same group have different queries and different patterns but read identical keys and values. The KV cache of lesson 12 shrinks by the ratio n_q/n_kv, which is why the design exists.</p>
<p>For a head claim this means "head 7's value vectors" is shorthand for "key/value head 7 // group_size's value vectors," shared with its siblings; an ablation of one query head's output does not remove the information from its group-mates.</p>
<h2 id="qk-norm">QK-Norm and no biases</h2>
<p>Qwen3 applies RMSNorm to each query and key vector over the head dimension before the rotation; the installed code has <code>q_norm</code> and <code>k_norm</code> of size head_dim '''+cite('hf-llama')+'''. This bounds the attention scores, since normalised vectors have fixed length. It also means the raw <em>W_Q W_K</em>ᵀ product is further from the score the model actually uses: there is a normalisation and a rotation between them. Removing the biases (both families) simplifies one thing: the stream is a sum of pure matrix products, with no constant vectors added per layer.</p>
<h2 id="tooling">What changes for the tools</h2>
<p>TransformerLens's config carries fields for each of these: <code>normalization_type</code> (LN or RMS), <code>positional_embedding_type</code> (standard or rotary), <code>gated_mlp</code>, <code>n_key_value_heads</code>, and <code>use_qk_norm</code>; for GPT-2 the bridge reports LN, standard, False, None, and False, and the hook names in lesson 13 are the ones to expect for GPT-2. For a Llama-style model the same residual and pattern hooks exist, there is no positional-embedding hook, <code>mlp.hook_post</code> is the gated product, and keys and values carry fewer heads than queries until expanded. In nnsight, module paths follow the Hugging Face class: <code>model.model.layers[i]</code>, <code>.self_attn</code>, <code>.mlp</code>, <code>.input_layernorm</code>, rather than <code>transformer.h[i]</code>. Read the printed module tree before writing a path.</p>
<div class="evals"><span class="label">In interpretability work</span><p>Port a method one lesson at a time. A neuron result moves to Llama with one change (hook the gated product); a QK-circuit result needs rethinking (rotation per offset); a direct-logit-attribution result needs the RMSNorm scale instead of the LayerNorm scale and no bias term. Write the GPT-2 version first, match it to 1e-4 as in lesson 10, then change one component and re-verify against the installed model code rather than against a diagram.</p></div>
<div class="pitfall"><span class="label">Pitfall</span><p>Assuming a model is "Llama-style" from its name. Model families differ in details that break tooling: tied or untied embeddings by size (Qwen3 switches at 8B), the presence of QK-Norm, attention biases (present in Qwen2, removed in Qwen3). The config file and the modeling source in the installed package are the ground truth; the paper is a summary.</p></div>''',
 'You have a GPT-2 experiment that projects every head\'s W_V W_O onto the unembedding to find heads that promote a token, and it uses hook_pos_embed as a baseline. Which parts carry over to Qwen3-0.6B unchanged, which need adjustment, and which have no equivalent?',
 'W_V W_O per query head carries over, with the caveat that W_V belongs to a key/value head shared by two query heads, so the matrix repeats within a group. Projection onto the unembedding carries over; the final normalisation is RMSNorm, so the scale factor changes form. hook_pos_embed has no equivalent: there is no position vector in the stream. Any baseline built from it must be replaced, for example by the embedding alone, and the write-up should say the baseline differs.',
 lab(['python labs/anatomy.py layernorm-demo'],
 'The RMSNorm line of the layer-norm demo is the only variant computation this course can run without a second model cached. Recompute it, then open the installed modeling_llama.py and modeling_qwen3.py and locate the five operations in the table: the norm, rotate_half, repeat_kv, the MLP forward line, and q_norm/k_norm.',
 '√((1 + 9 + 25 + 49)/4) = 4.5826 and [1, 3, 5, 7]/4.5826 = [0.2182, 0.6547, 1.0911, 1.5275]. In the installed source, LlamaRMSNorm.forward, rotate_half, apply_rotary_pos_emb, repeat_kv, and LlamaMLP.forward are each a few lines; Qwen3Attention adds q_norm and k_norm over head_dim. If any of the five looks different in your installed version, that difference is the fact to record.'),
 ['llama','qwen3','rope','rmsnorm','gqa','hf-llama','tl-compat'], kw='Llama Qwen3 RMSNorm rotary RoPE SwiGLU grouped-query attention GQA QK-Norm variants'),

lesson(15,4,'readiness-review','Readiness review',
 'The test is concrete. This page is the rubric for it, a checklist of what you should now be able to say without looking, and the handoff to the interpretability course.',
 '''<h2 id="the-test">The test</h2>
<p>Implement a GPT-2 forward pass from raw weights, match the reference logits, then re-derive the same activations with TransformerLens or nnsight. In this course that is three commands:</p>'''+term(["python -m unittest discover -s labs -p 'test_*.py' -v",'python labs/anatomy.py compare --tools','python labs/anatomy.py names'])+'''
<p>With the stubs unimplemented, the test suite reports errors (the transcript records that state); with a correct implementation all 24 tests pass, and the comparison prints every max-difference at or below 1e-4 against Hugging Face, TransformerLens in no-processing mode, and nnsight.</p>
<h2 id="rubric">Rubric</h2>
<table><tr><th>Criterion</th><th>Ready</th><th>Not yet</th></tr>
<tr><td>Forward pass</td><td>Logits within 1e-3 of Hugging Face on the test prompt and on a prompt of your own choosing; argmax agrees at every position; you can name the cause of any residual discrepancy</td><td>Argmax agrees but tolerance fails; or passes only on one prompt</td></tr>
<tr><td>Cache</td><td>resid_pre/mid/post, pattern, attn_out, mlp_post, mlp_out for every block, matching the library values; stream identity holds</td><td>Only logits implemented; or cache keys present but unchecked</td></tr>
<tr><td>Naming</td><td>Given any row of the lesson-13 table, you can write the Hugging Face, TransformerLens, and nnsight access for it, including the [0] on attention output, the hidden_states offset, and the ln_f trap</td><td>Needs the table open; confuses hidden_states[12] with resid_post.11</td></tr>
<tr><td>Hand computation</td><td>Can redo the three-token attention example, the [1, 3, 5, 7] layer norm, the two-dimensional SwiGLU, and the [2, 1, 0] softmax with a pencil and get the printed numbers</td><td>Can read the outputs but not reproduce them</td></tr>
<tr><td>Shapes</td><td>Writes (b, s, 768), (b, 12, s, s), (b, s, 12, 64), (b, s, 3072), (b, s, 50257) from memory and asserts them in code</td><td>Looks them up; transposes slip through</td></tr>
<tr><td>Counting</td><td>Derives 124,439,808 and the GPT-2 medium figure from the config</td><td>Quotes 124M</td></tr>
<tr><td>Tokens</td><td>States claims in token ids; checks span alignment; knows " the" ≠ "the" ≠ "The"</td><td>Talks about words</td></tr>
<tr><td>Variants</td><td>For RMSNorm, RoPE, SwiGLU, and GQA, says what changes in which lesson and what the tooling consequence is</td><td>Treats all decoder models as GPT-2</td></tr>
<tr><td>Provenance</td><td>Every architectural fact you state about a model comes with "from the config," "from the modeling source," or a citation</td><td>Numbers from memory</td></tr></table>
<h2 id="say-without-looking">Ten things to say without looking</h2>
<ol>
<li>The signature: ids (b, s) → logits (b, s, 50257); the stream in between is (b, s, 768).</li>
<li>The first residual vector is wte[id] + wpe[pos]; nothing else happens before block 0.</li>
<li>The stream is a sum of 1 + 24 writes; the norms do not add; position 0 is an outlier in this checkpoint.</li>
<li>Pre-LN: ln_1 before attention, ln_2 before the MLP, ln_f before the unembedding; the stream is never normalised in place; LN is scale-invariant and deletes the all-ones direction.</li>
<li>Attention: scores q·k/√64, mask with −∞, softmax rows sum to 1, output is a convex mix of values.</li>
<li>Two circuits: W_Q W_Kᵀ decides where, W_V W_O decides what, each rank ≤ 64 in a 768-space.</li>
<li>A neuron is a column of W_in, a scalar after GELU, and a row of W_out; the hidden basis is privileged, the stream's is not.</li>
<li>SwiGLU is (SiLU(xW) ⊙ xV)W₂; used by Llama and Qwen3; a neuron then has two input directions.</li>
<li>Logits are ln_f(stream) · wteᵀ; the logit lens applies the same ending earlier; softmax ignores a constant shift.</li>
<li>TransformerLens 4 loads with boot_transformers + enable_compatibility_mode; default mode folds and centres; no_processing matches Hugging Face; nnsight needs save(), execution order, and a file.</li></ol>
<h2 id="what-next">What comes next</h2>
<p>The <a href="../../../interpretability/interpretability-tut/index.html">interpretability course</a> uses everything here. Its first language-model replication reads a neuron under <code>blocks.N.mlp.hook_post</code> and an attention head under <code>blocks.N.attn.hook_pattern</code>, and its rule, from the <a href="../../../evals/evals-tut/index.html">evals course</a> and the <a href="../../../statistics/statistics-tut/index.html">statistics course</a>, is that the prediction test is written before the look: say what the activation will do on held-out data, say what control would also pass if the explanation were empty, then run. The anatomy on these pages is what makes those sentences precise: which tensor, which position, which token id, which library, which processing mode.</p>
<h2 id="for-tutors">For tutors</h2>
<p><code>labs/ASSESSMENTS.md</code> describes held-out variant families for each lab (different prompts, different layers, perturbed hand examples, and modified-architecture forward passes) without publishing them, following the repository's assessment contract. A learner who passes the published tests should be given one variant from each family.</p>
<div class="evals"><span class="label">In interpretability work</span><p>The readiness test is a test of your ability to be wrong in a detectable way. A forward pass that matches to 1e-4 is one you can modify for an experiment and still trust; an activation you can locate in three libraries is one whose provenance you can defend in a write-up. That is the whole purpose of anatomy.</p></div>
<div class="pitfall"><span class="label">Pitfall</span><p>Declaring readiness after the tests pass once. Run the comparison on a prompt you chose, with a different length, and on a batch of two if you extended the implementation that far. The tests are a floor.</p></div>''',
 'Your implementation passes every test. A reviewer asks for one piece of evidence that it is correct rather than fitted to the test prompt. What do you show?',
 'The compare command on a prompt the tests never used, with its max-differences at the same 1e-4 scale and argmax agreement, plus the names table at a layer other than 5 showing your cache agrees with both libraries. Because the implementation has no prompt-specific code paths and the comparison is against an independent implementation, agreement on a fresh prompt is evidence about the code rather than about the prompt.',
 lab(['python labs/anatomy.py compare --tools "Mechanistic interpretability asks what a model computes, not only what it outputs."'],
 'Run the comparison on this longer prompt (eighteen tokens) with your implementation. Record the maximum differences.',
 'With the reference implementation this prompt gives a logit discrepancy of about 9e-5 against a largest logit near 197, and residual discrepancies around 1e-4; your numbers should be of the same order. If they are larger by a factor of ten or more, something is systematically off even though the short prompt passed.'),
 ['elhage','tl-migration','nnsight-quickstart'], kw='readiness review rubric checklist assessment'),
]
